[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608130100"
  :adr/title "Security first replaces local first; review becomes per-provider and universal"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-13"
  :adr/supersedes "the local-first framing in README and bots.clj"
  :adr/body
  "## Decision

Cloud Itonami's founding principle is SECURITY first, not LOCAL first (owner
directive, 2026-08-13). Keeping data on this machine remains an instrument for
security. It stops being the thing that grants permission.

`policy.kotoba/provider-allowed?` changes from

    enabled AND (local OR (cloud-enabled AND cloud-reviewed))

to

    enabled AND reviewed AND (no-egress OR (egress-permitted AND confidential AND authenticated))

## Why the old rule was the principle's bug, not its expression

Two things were wrong with it, and only one of them was about the cloud.

FIRST: `local` was a free pass. An enabled loopback provider was admitted on
that alone. But a process listening on 127.0.0.1 is close, not trustworthy —
any local process can bind a port, and 'the bytes did not leave the machine'
and 'this is safe' are different sentences. The old rule substituted one for
the other, which is exactly what a security-first principle must not do.

SECOND, and the reason this surfaced: the ONLY way to admit a cloud provider
was the global `:privacy :allow-cloud-without-review?`. There was no way to
record that a specific provider HAD been reviewed. An operator who had
actually reviewed one destination had to switch review off for everything in
order to use it. A gate whose only setting is 'skip me' is not a review gate,
and its existence is what told us the principle was upside down rather than
merely strict.

## What replaces it

Review is UNIVERSAL and per provider. `:reviewed?` is a positive fact about one
destination: somebody looked at this and said yes. It is the whole gate for a
provider with no egress and half the gate for one with egress.

Egress adds what egress needs: `:cloud-enabled?` (a deployment-wide switch, so
an operator can forbid all egress in one place), an encrypted transport, and a
credential that is actually exported. The credential is not about
confidentiality — TLS already provides that — but about the destination being
an account somebody holds rather than a URL somebody typed.

The result is STRICTER overall, which is the test of whether this was a
loosening dressed up as a principle. Before, an enabled loopback provider was
admitted; now it must also be reviewed. What became possible is narrow and
deliberate: a reviewed, encrypted, credentialed cloud provider — for example
the murakumo fleet — can be admitted, which under the old rule required
declaring that review does not happen.

## Facts are derived, never declared

The host computes every fact from the configuration rather than reading a flag:
`no-egress` by parsing the host out of `:base-url`, `confidential` from its
scheme, `authenticated` by reading the named environment variable. `:local?`
survives as documentation and is no longer an input — a provider that called
itself local while pointing at a remote address is precisely the case a flag
cannot catch, and precisely what a typo or an attacker gets to write.

## Shipped defaults stay fail-closed

The distribution is tenant-neutral, so it reviews nothing on a stranger's
behalf. Loopback providers ship `:reviewed? true` because the distribution can
vouch for 'the machine you are already running this on' and for nothing else.
Every provider that leaves the machine — murakumo and OpenAI both — ships
`:reviewed? false`, `:enabled? false`, with `:cloud-enabled?` false. Turning
those on is the act of reviewing, and it happens in an operator's own config.

## Consequences

- `:privacy :allow-cloud-without-review?` is retired. It cannot be expressed in
  the new rule and should not be: it named the absence of the thing that now
  admits a provider.
- The UI stopped asserting 「ローカルモード」 unconditionally. That badge was
  true only while egress was impossible; with a reviewed provider admitted it
  claimed the opposite of what the application was doing.
- `core-test/local-first-policy-is-fail-closed` became
  `security-first-policy-is-fail-closed`. The assertions keep their shape; what
  admits a provider is what changed.
- Every provider fixture in the suite had to gain `:reviewed?` and a real
  `:base-url`. That churn is the rule being enforced rather than described.

## Verification

The parity corpus went from 16 rows to 32 x the egress switch, exercised
through the derivation rather than by setting the booleans directly — a fixture
that set them would test the core twice and the derivation not at all. Four
properties are asserted separately from agreement: an unreviewed loopback
provider is denied (TRUE before this change, which was the bug); plaintext
egress is denied; a named-but-unexported credential does not count as one; and
a provider that declares `:local? true` while pointing at a remote host is
treated as egress.

Watched failing: inverting the review test admitted an unreviewed provider and
failed `security-first-review-is-universal`, the oracle case table, and the
ClojureScript gate. 1403 tests / 8372 assertions on the JVM and 106 oracle
cases over 8 cores on ClojureScript, both green."}]
